---
import { waHref } from '../data/whatsapp.js';
// VISUAILS — /how-it-works, rewritten. AUDIT-TASK-0.md §F: "REWRITE. 'One
// photo in. A campaign out.' → drop-shaped. All ~24h claims corrected."
//
// §B·3 supplies the frame: "'One photo in' is the per-product story; the batch
// story is 'one folder in'." So the page is shaped around a whole order moving
// through the pipeline, not a single upload.
//
// ── WHAT THE AUGUST 2026 PRICING MODEL CHANGED ON THIS PAGE ────────────────
//
// Section 0 of src/data/pricing.js is the governing document. Two things here
// were falsified by it, and both were structural rather than cosmetic:
//
//   1 · THE PAGE PRESENTED TWO PRODUCTS. "A drop" did one thing and "individual
//       products" did another — a portal against a download link, a reserved
//       window against a queue — and the reader was left to work out which of
//       the two they were buying. That was accurate under the package model,
//       where the buyer picked a size and a service level as two separate
//       questions. It is wrong now. There is ONE thing to order, and the
//       service level follows its size: from WINDOW_THRESHOLD products the
//       order is in the capacity calendar with the reserved window, below it
//       the standard queue. Every place this page used to name two products it
//       now names one line, read from WINDOW_THRESHOLD rather than described,
//       so /pricing and this page cannot give different accounts of the same
//       gate.
//   2 · The scope constant (a Full Drop's 25–30 products) is gone with the
//       package it described. Where the page needed it — the consistency claim
//       — it now says "every product in the order", which is true at any count
//       and does not have to be re-edited when a band moves.
//
// The word "drop" itself is retired from the copy: it means a collection going
// live to the brands we sell to, and this page used it for a work order in its
// own H1. EN says an order or a batch; NL says een bestelling. It survives only
// where a CLIENT's launch is meant, which on this page is nowhere.
//
// Two rules govern every timing claim below:
//   • Task #190 — the ~24-hour promise is retired. Nothing here types a
//     duration; the two sanctioned strings come from turnaround() in
//     pricing.js, one per tier.
//   • The brief's capacity rule — "never promise a delivery date the capacity
//     gate hasn't cleared". The gate is not a footnote on this page, it is
//     step 02 and then a block of its own, because it is the promise that
//     makes every other promise here believable.
//
// The four steps deliberately echo /start rather than reinventing the account:
// /start and /how-it-works describe one pipeline and must not drift apart.
// Where the wording expands here, it expands — it never contradicts.
//
// A CORRECTION TO THAT PARAGRAPH, which used to name "StartPage.astro's `flow`
// array". There is no `flow` array in StartPage.astro. What is there is
// FORM.rail — ['Scope', 'Material', 'Brief', 'Timing', 'Confirm'] — and those
// are the ORDER FORM's five steps, not the service's four. The two lists are
// adjacent, not the same: rail 1–3 all happen inside "you send the products",
// rail 4 is the capacity gate, and rail 5 comes before any production starts.
// So the rule this comment states was pointing at a name that could not be
// checked, which is the same as not pointing anywhere. It names the real thing
// now, and the rule itself is unchanged and still binding.
//
// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// THE FIGURES (task #246). Lucas: the sections lack variation, the page reads
// long, and the site explains its services in words where it should be showing
// them.
//
// This paragraph used to open "measured before this change: 8 sections, 4 of
// them structurally identical to a neighbour, 505 words against 2 images — one
// of which is a hero background". One of those claims is false and it is
// corrected here rather than dropped, because a comment quietly losing a claim
// reads the same as a claim that was always true:
//
//   • "4 structurally identical to a neighbour" — FALSE, and it flattered the
//     change. Signing each section by its layout, container shape then which
//     devices it carries (heading band, card grid, two-column split, figure,
//     CTA band), the page at HEAD had EIGHT distinct signatures and ZERO
//     adjacent repeats. The sections were not repeating each other; they were
//     all prose in a container, which is a different complaint and the one
//     Lucas made. Writing "identical" was me reaching for the DESIGN.md phrase
//     "identical card grids" because it was nearby, not because I had looked.
//
//   • "8 sections" and "2 images, one a hero background" — both true. HEAD
//     referenced exactly two files: /img/banners-04.webp behind the hero, and
//     /img/lifestyle-glow-06.webp, passed to <Compare> as both before AND
//     after. That second one is not a copy-paste slip: the call passes `grade`,
//     and Compare.astro's own header defines grade mode as one photo for both
//     sides with the before auto-desaturated, "for the styles where we have one
//     finished photo, not a separate raw one". /gallery uses it four more
//     times. I had this drafted as a logged defect before reading the
//     component.
//
//   • "505 words" — near enough. A recount gives 482, and the gap is method,
//     not error: this one counts every string in COPY.en of twelve characters
//     or more, a crude floor that keeps class names and hrefs out and takes a
//     few short lines with them. Quoted as "words of visible copy" for that
//     reason, and any comparison has to be run the same way on both sides.
//
// Correcting that one claim produced two more before it landed, and they
// belong in the block whose whole subject is comments outliving the checking
// behind them. I first wrote that HEAD "referenced no images at all", from a
// probe reporting zero — it matched an allow-list of attribute spellings
// (photo=, made=, src=, image=) and HEAD's two references are a CSS url() and
// props called before=/after=, so both read as absence. Then I wrote the
// duplicate before/after up as a defect, which took twelve lines of
// Compare.astro to disprove — twelve lines I had not read when I wrote it.
//
// One shape under all three: a claim about the code, written from something
// adjacent to the code — a phrase from DESIGN.md, a tool's silence, an
// asymmetry that looked wrong. The silence is the dangerous one and the fix is
// filed in rhythm_probe.py: match the DESTINATION, /img/, not the syntax
// around it, because a checker enumerating the spellings it knows reports
// every unknown one as nothing-there, and nothing-there is the answer nobody
// goes back to verify.
//
// What was true and stays true: the page had no explanatory figure of any
// kind. It has five now, and two of them REPLACE prose rather than sitting
// beside it — FigPipeline takes over the four numbered step cards (which also
// retires the 01/02/03/04 scaffolding DESIGN.md rejects), and FigApproval
// takes over the portal's four-item checklist. That is 57 words of prose
// deleted. Section count 8 → 9, adjacent repeats 0 → 0, image files 2 → 4.
//
// The sentence that followed — "adding pictures while keeping every word would
// have made the page longer, which is the opposite of what was asked for" —
// implied the page ended up shorter. It did not, and the same count says so.
// EN, HEAD → now: visible copy 482 → 639. Split by what a reader actually
// meets: alt text 16 → 49, figcaptions 0 → 101, body prose 466 → 489. NL moves
// the same way, 501 → 671, with alt 19 → 46, figcaptions 0 → 109 and body
// prose 482 → 516.
//
// FOUR OF THOSE EIGHT NUMBERS ARE CORRECTIONS, and the correction is worth more
// than the numbers. The first version of this paragraph read "alt text 16 → 56,
// figcaptions 8 → 109, body prose 458 → 474", straight off copy_census.py,
// which at the time sorted a string into a bucket by the SHAPE OF ITS KEY —
// anything matching (alt|aria|label) was accessibility surface, anything
// matching (note|caption) was a figure's note. That is a vocabulary rule
// wearing a heuristic's clothes, and it broke on the next page it met:
// /compare reported 50 words of alt text on a page with no alt text on it, and
// 42 of those 50 are the matrix's visible row headers and a figure's visible
// lane labels, both called `label`. Here it was milder and in the same
// direction — "figcaptions 8" at HEAD, on a revision with no figures in it at
// all, is `portalNote`, a paragraph under the portal section that a suffix
// mistook for a drawing's caption. The census now asks the TEMPLATE which
// attribute a key is bound to, so the buckets follow use rather than naming.
//
// The direction is the part to keep: every one of those errors moved words OUT
// of body prose, which is the column #246 is judged on, so the page read as
// leaner than it was. A tool that is wrong in the flattering direction is one
// nobody has a reason to go back and check.
//
// So the honest position on the half of #246 that says "too much text": body
// prose is close to flat, +23 words EN and +34 NL, and the growth is +101 words
// of figcaption. +33 of the rest is alt text, which no sighted reader meets and
// which five new figures necessarily add.
//
// The +101 is already the trimmed number. The first drafts of these captions
// came to 188 words — a picture, then a paragraph explaining the picture,
// which is the reflex this task exists to answer — and that 188 is the one
// figure on this page nobody can re-measure, because the drafts were never
// committed. Take it as the note it is, not as a number like the others. What
// survives the trim is only what a drawing cannot say about itself, and the
// reasoning for each cut sits above its caption in COPY, where it can be
// argued with.
//
// Anyone re-measuring should split the same three ways before comparing, or a
// page will look like it grew when what grew was its accessibility surface.
//
// All figure copy lives in the COPY table below, in both branches, so
// check_lang_parity holds EN/NL parity on it exactly as it does for prose.
// Drawing constants — ratios, block widths, tile patterns, glyph paths — live
// inside the figure components, because they are the same in every language.
import Compare from './Compare.astro';
// FigFanOut, FigPipeline, FigCapacity, FigApproval and FigFormats used to be
// imported here, and then FigFlow was, for about a day. All six are gone and
// all six files still exist, imported by nothing; deleting them is a separate,
// reversible decision and this page is not the place to make it.
//
// FigFlow put all three services on screen at once, as switches you could
// combine, with a product-count slider and the capacity gate. Lucas asked for
// the opposite — ONE service followed from the phone photo to the files, the
// other two not on screen at all ("Ieder apart van elkaar") — and for the
// slider and the gate to go: the gate is stated in prose below and the prices
// live on /pricing. FigWalk is that, and /demo shows the same component, so
// there is one walkthrough and one set of photographs to shoot for it.
import FigWalk from './FigWalk.astro';
import {
  TIERS, reviewClaim, turnaround, aftercare, euro, AMOUNT,
  WINDOW_THRESHOLD, vatLabel, vatNote, TEST_SAMPLE,
} from '../data/pricing.js';
import { localizedPath } from '../i18n/ui.js';

interface Props { lang: 'en' | 'nl'; }
const { lang } = Astro.props;
const lp = (p: string) => localizedPath(lang, p);

// The test sample is the only price this page prints, and it now prints with a
// VAT label attached — BRIEF-14's rule, and it was being broken here quietly:
// a bare €0.99 next to "no commitment" reads as the whole of what leaves the
// account, and it is not. Both figures are built here rather than in the copy
// table so neither branch can print one without the other.
const sample = `${euro(AMOUNT.testSample, lang)} ${vatLabel('excl', lang)}`;

// No `as const` — see PricingPage.astro for why (union-of-tuples breaks .map).
const COPY = {
  en: {
    h1a: 'One folder in.',
    h1b: 'A whole ',
    h1em: 'batch',
    h1c: ' out.',
    lead: `You send a folder of product photos and a short note. We give the order a window the calendar can actually hold, produce it, check it by hand, and hand it back image by image.`,
    ctaStart: 'Start an order',
    ctaSample: 'Try a test sample',

    // ── FIGWALK ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    // Two keys, where FigFlow needed eighteen. Everything the walkthrough says
    // — step headings, style names, the model line, the caption admitting the
    // images are stand-ins — lives in WALK_COPY in src/data/demo.js, because
    // /demo renders the same component and copy kept in two page files is copy
    // that drifts. These two are this page's own framing of it.
    flowH: 'Your order, end to end.',
    flowLede: 'One service at a time, from the phone photo you send to the files that come back. Pick one and scroll: the window holds still and shows what exists at that moment.',
    gateLead: 'No date is printed until the calendar clears it.',
    gateBody: 'A reserved window is confirmed before you pay, never after. If the week you need cannot be held, you hear that — not an optimistic guess. It is the one promise on this site we would rather lose an order over than break.',
    craftH: 'More than AI. Finished by hand.',
    craftBody: "The pipeline does the heavy lifting, but it is only the start. Every visual is finished in professional editing tools and colour-graded to your brand, and a person checks accuracy, consistency and framing before anything ships — the difference between a raw generator and something you would publish.",
    chips: ['Professional editing & retouch', 'Colour grading to your brand', reviewClaim('attended', 'en')],
    beforeAlt: 'A flat, ungraded product scene before VISUAILS finishing',
    afterAlt: 'The same scene, finished and human-checked by VISUAILS',

    portalH: 'You approve it, image by image.',
    portalBody: `An order of ${WINDOW_THRESHOLD} products or more does not arrive as a zip file and a hope. ${TIERS.attended.delivery.en}`,
    portalNote: `Smaller orders work differently and more simply. ${TIERS.unattended.delivery.en}.`,

    detailsH: 'The details that matter',
    details: [
      { t: 'Turnaround', b: `From ${WINDOW_THRESHOLD} products: ${turnaround('attended', 'en').toLowerCase()}. Below that: ${turnaround('unattended', 'en').toLowerCase()}, ${TIERS.unattended.queue.en.toLowerCase()}.` },
      { t: 'If something is off', b: `From ${WINDOW_THRESHOLD} products: ${aftercare('attended', 'en').toLowerCase()}. Below that: ${aftercare('unattended', 'en').toLowerCase()}.` },
      // No product band any more: the count is whatever you ordered, and the
      // claim is true at every count, so naming one would only date the page.
      { t: 'Consistency', b: 'Same lighting, same angle, same grade across every product in the order. Running them together is what makes that possible.' },
      { t: 'Two ways in', b: 'On-site, or WhatsApp — whichever suits you. The same pipeline either way.' },
      { t: 'Your rights', b: 'Full commercial usage rights to everything we deliver.', linkText: 'Read the terms', linkHref: '/terms' },
    ],
    faqH: 'Still have a question?',
    // The second inbound link to /guides (brief §1 — it had none in any page
    // body). It sits under the FAQ heading because that is where someone with
    // a question already is, and it says which questions the FAQ does not take.
    faqGuides: `The FAQ answers the practical ones; the longer reads — how to shoot the photo you send us, what a shoot day actually costs — are in the <a href="${lp('/guides')}">guides</a>.`,
    faqCta: 'Read the FAQ',

    ctaH: 'See it on your own product.',
    ctaBody: `One test sample per business — ${sample}, no commitment. It runs through the same pipeline as a paid order.`,
    ctaSampleBtn: `One image · ${TEST_SAMPLE.en.price}`,
    ctaStartBtn: 'Start an order',
    wa: waHref("Hi VISUAILS, I have a question about how an order works."),
  },

  nl: {
    h1a: 'Eén map erin.',
    h1b: 'Een hele ',
    h1em: 'bestelling',
    h1c: ' eruit.',
    lead: 'Jij stuurt een map met productfoto’s en een korte notitie. Wij geven de bestelling een venster dat de agenda ook echt kan vasthouden, produceren hem, controleren met de hand, en geven hem beeld voor beeld terug.',
    ctaStart: 'Start een bestelling',
    ctaSample: 'Probeer een proefvisual',

    flowH: 'Je bestelling, van begin tot eind.',
    flowLede: 'Eén dienst tegelijk, van de telefoonfoto die je stuurt tot de bestanden die terugkomen. Kies er één en scroll: het venster blijft staan en laat zien wat er op dat moment bestaat.',
    gateLead: 'Er wordt geen datum genoemd voordat de agenda die heeft vrijgegeven.',
    gateBody: 'Een gereserveerd venster wordt bevestigd voordat je betaalt, nooit erna. Kan de week die je nodig hebt niet worden vastgehouden, dan hoor je dat — geen optimistische gok. Het is de ene belofte op deze site waarvoor we liever een bestelling verliezen dan haar te breken.',
    craftH: 'Meer dan AI. Met de hand afgewerkt.',
    craftBody: 'De pipeline doet het zware werk, maar dat is pas het begin. Elke visual wordt afgewerkt in professionele editingtools en kleurgecorrigeerd naar je merk, en een mens controleert nauwkeurigheid, consistentie en kadrering voordat er iets weggaat — het verschil tussen een kale generator en iets dat je zou publiceren.',
    chips: ['Professionele editing & retouche', 'Kleurcorrectie naar je merk', reviewClaim('attended', 'nl')],
    beforeAlt: 'Een platte, ongecorrigeerde productscène vóór de afwerking van VISUAILS',
    afterAlt: 'Dezelfde scène, afgewerkt en met de hand gecontroleerd door VISUAILS',

    portalH: 'Jij keurt goed, beeld voor beeld.',
    portalBody: `Een bestelling van ${WINDOW_THRESHOLD} producten of meer komt niet aan als een zipbestand en een goede hoop. ${TIERS.attended.delivery.nl}`,
    portalNote: `Kleinere bestellingen werken anders en simpeler. ${TIERS.unattended.delivery.nl}.`,

    detailsH: 'De details die ertoe doen',
    details: [
      { t: 'Levertijd', b: `Vanaf ${WINDOW_THRESHOLD} producten: ${turnaround('attended', 'nl').toLowerCase()}. Daaronder: ${turnaround('unattended', 'nl').toLowerCase()}, ${TIERS.unattended.queue.nl.toLowerCase()}.` },
      { t: 'Als iets niet klopt', b: `Vanaf ${WINDOW_THRESHOLD} producten: ${aftercare('attended', 'nl').toLowerCase()}. Daaronder: ${aftercare('unattended', 'nl').toLowerCase()}.` },
      // Zie de EN-tak: geen productbereik meer, de claim klopt bij elk aantal.
      { t: 'Consistentie', b: 'Dezelfde belichting, dezelfde hoek, dezelfde grade over elk product in de bestelling. Dat ze samen door de pipeline gaan is precies wat dat mogelijk maakt.' },
      { t: 'Twee ingangen', b: 'Via de site of via WhatsApp — wat jou uitkomt. Dezelfde pipeline in beide gevallen.' },
      { t: 'Jouw rechten', b: 'Volledige commerciële gebruiksrechten op alles wat we leveren.', linkText: 'Lees de voorwaarden', linkHref: '/terms' },
    ],
    faqH: 'Nog een vraag?',
    faqGuides: `De FAQ beantwoordt de praktische vragen; de langere stukken — hoe je de foto maakt die je ons stuurt, wat een shootdag echt kost — staan bij de <a href="${lp('/guides')}">gidsen</a>.`,
    faqCta: 'Lees de FAQ',

    ctaH: 'Zie het op je eigen product.',
    ctaBody: `Eén proefvisual per bedrijf — ${sample}, zonder verplichting. Hij loopt door dezelfde pipeline als een betaalde bestelling.`,
    ctaSampleBtn: `Eén beeld · ${TEST_SAMPLE.nl.price}`,
    ctaStartBtn: 'Start een bestelling',
    wa: waHref("Hoi VISUAILS, ik heb een vraag over hoe een bestelling werkt."),
  },
};

const c = COPY[lang];

---

<!-- HERO -->
<section class="hero-editorial compact">
  <div class="bg" style="background-image:url('/img/banners-04.webp')"></div>
  <div class="fade"></div>
  <div class="hero-editorial-content container">
    <h1 class="display" data-split-lines>{c.h1a}<br />{c.h1b}<em>{c.h1em}</em>{c.h1c}</h1>
    <p class="lead" style="margin-top:1.2rem;max-width:60ch">{c.lead}</p>
    <div class="flex" style="margin-top:1.8rem">
      <span class="magnet-wrap"><span class="magnet-inner"><a href={lp('/start')} class="btn btn-primary btn-lg" data-track="hiw-start">{c.ctaStart}</a></span></span>
      <a href={lp('/test-sample')} class="btn btn-ghost btn-lg">{c.ctaSample}</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ONE ORDER, FOLLOWED FROM END TO END. This section is what six were: the
     five figures the page carried, and then FigFlow, which replaced them for a
     day. What changed between FigFlow and this is not the drawing but the
     question it answers. FigFlow put all three services on screen as switches
     you could combine — a calculator, good for finding a price and useless for
     understanding a process, because there is no order and no story in it.
     This follows ONE service, and while you are in it the other two are not on
     the page. Lucas: "Ieder apart van elkaar."

     The gate promise stays in prose above it, where it has always been. It is
     the one claim on this page we would rather lose an order over than break,
     and it belongs in a sentence a reader cannot skim past rather than in a
     node on a diagram they might. -->
<section class="section-tight"><div class="container">
  <div class="section-head"><h2>{c.flowH}</h2></div>
  <p class="flow-lede reveal pending">{c.flowLede}</p>
  <div class="gate reveal pending">
    <p class="editorial-statement">{c.gateLead}</p>
    <p class="gate-body">{c.gateBody}</p>
  </div>
  <div class="reveal pending">
    <FigWalk lang={lang} />
  </div>
</div></section>

<!-- FINISHED BY HAND -->
<section class="section-tight"><div class="container two-col">
  <div class="reveal pending">
    <Compare
      grade
      before="/img/lifestyle-glow-06.webp"
      after="/img/lifestyle-glow-06.webp"
      beforeAlt={c.beforeAlt}
      afterAlt={c.afterAlt}
    />
  </div>
  <div class="reveal pending">
    <h2>{c.craftH}</h2>
    <p class="lead" style="margin-top:1.1rem">{c.craftBody}</p>
    <div class="chip-row" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      {c.chips.map((chip) => (
        <span class="chip"><span class="dot"></span>{chip}</span>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div>
</div></section>

<!-- THE PORTAL, IN WORDS. The drawing of it — three states at once — is now
     the delivery stage of the rail above, so what is left here is the claim
     itself. One column, not two: a two-column block with nothing in the right
     half is a layout waiting for an image that is not coming. -->
<section class="section-tight"><div class="container narrow">
  <div class="reveal pending">
    <h2>{c.portalH}</h2>
    <p class="lead" style="margin-top:1.1rem">{c.portalBody}</p>
    <p class="portal-note">{c.portalNote}</p>
  </div>
</div></section>

<!-- THE DETAILS. FigFormats and its heading used to open this section; the five
     channel crops are the fan-out at the foot of the rail now, and the
     high-resolution claim that was once a sixth card travelled with them into
     the rail's caption. What is left is the card grid it always ended with. -->
<section><div class="container">
  <div class="section-head"><h2>{c.detailsH}</h2></div>
  <div class="grid grid-auto reveal-group">
    {c.details.map((d) => (
      <div class="card reveal pending">
        <h3 style="font-size:1.05rem">{d.t}</h3>
        <p style="margin-top:.5rem;font-size:.95rem;color:var(--ink-3)">{d.b}{d.linkText ? <> <a href={lp(d.linkHref)}>{d.linkText}</a>.</> : null}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
</div></section>

<!-- FAQ -->
<section class="section-tight"><div class="container">
  <div style="text-align:center;max-width:60ch;margin:0 auto 1.4rem">
    <h2>{c.faqH}</h2>
    <p class="faq-guides" set:html={c.faqGuides} />
  </div>
  <div class="flex" style="justify-content:center"><a href={lp('/faq')} class="btn btn-ghost btn-lg">{c.faqCta}</a></div>
</div></section>

<!-- CTA -->
<section class="section-tight"><div class="container">
  <div class="cta-band reveal pending">
    <h2 class="display" style="font-size:clamp(2rem,4.5vw,3.2rem)">{c.ctaH}</h2>
    <p class="lead" style="margin:1rem auto 0;text-align:center">{c.ctaBody}</p>
    <div class="flex" style="justify-content:center;margin-top:1.8rem">
      <a href={lp('/test-sample')} class="btn btn-primary btn-lg" data-track="hiw-sample">{c.ctaSampleBtn}</a>
      <a href={lp('/start')} class="btn btn-ghost btn-lg">{c.ctaStartBtn}</a>
      <a href={c.wa} class="btn btn-wa" target="_blank" rel="noopener">WhatsApp</a>
    </div>
    {/* vatNote() ONCE PER PAGE, which this page had never said. Every figure it
        prints carries `excl. VAT` — BRIEF-14's per-number half — but the other
        half is one line saying how VAT is actually handled, the way HomeV2 and
        StartPage do. It sits here rather than under the walkthrough because
        this is where the page's one price is; a VAT line under a figure that
        quotes nothing is a footnote to nothing. */}
    <p class="cta-vat">{vatNote(lang)}</p>
  </div>
</div></section>

<style>
  /* The `.steps.four` override and `.step p a` that used to live here are gone
     with the markup they styled — FigPipeline replaced the four step cards, so
     the rules had nothing left to select. Deleting them is not tidying: an
     emitted-nowhere CSS rule is exactly what verify3 [5] reports as dead, and
     leaving it would have cost a real finding to make room for a false one.
     Global .steps / .step / .step-n stay put — six other pages use them. */

  /* The capacity gate. A single heavy rule above it and nothing else — the
     sentence is the design. */
  .gate { border-top: 2px solid var(--ink); padding-top: clamp(1.4rem, 3vw, 2.2rem); }
  .gate-body { margin-top: 1.1rem; color: var(--ink-2); max-width: 66ch; }
  /* `.gate-fig` used to be here and is gone with FigCapacity — see the note at
     the top of this block about not leaving a rule that selects nothing.
     The gate block now introduces the rail rather than a figure of its own, so
     what it needs is room underneath it, not around a drawing. */
